<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6"><p class="font-bold">The wheels turn together, but only the outer rim rolls on the ground.</p><svg class="w-full" viewBox="0 0 460 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="figure-title figure-desc"><title id="figure-title">The wheels turn together, but only the outer rim rolls on the ground.</title><desc id="figure-desc">Two concentric wheels start and end in the same relative positions. The outer circumference determines the distance moved. The inner wheel slides relative to an imaginary lower track.</desc><path d="M30 225 H430" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M30 190 H430" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6 5" opacity=".5"/><g fill="none" stroke-width="3"><circle cx="116" cy="155" r="70" stroke="#bd4b37"/><circle cx="116" cy="155" r="36" stroke="#387a91"/><circle cx="343" cy="155" r="70" stroke="#bd4b37"/><circle cx="343" cy="155" r="36" stroke="#387a91"/></g><g fill="currentColor" font-size="17" text-anchor="middle"><text x="116" y="58">Start</text><text x="343" y="58">After one turn</text><text x="228" y="255">Same forward distance for both rims</text></g><path d="M186 73 H271" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M264 67 L273 73 L264 79" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></svg><figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">The large rim rolls without slipping on the solid ground line. The smaller rim never touches that ground; compared with the dashed imaginary track, it slides. Sharing the same journey does not mean the two circumferences are equal.</figcaption></figure>